    "highlighterClass": "highlight-indigo",
    "title": "Dykstra\u2019s Alternating Projection Algorithm",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-1305\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><path d=\"M140.0,75.0 L234.0,128.0 L234.0,22.0\" fill=\"#d9dee7\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"52\" y1=\"28\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"128\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"52\" y1=\"122\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"22\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"9\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">C\u2081</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"9\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">C\u2082</text><g transform=\"translate(40.0 76.0) rotate(0) scale(0.8)\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><polygon points=\"12,0 5,-2.2 -9,-1.6 -9,1.6 5,2.2\"/><polygon points=\"3,0 -4,-10 -7,-10 -3,0 -7,10 -4,10\"/><polygon points=\"-8,0 -11,-4.5 -12.5,-4.5 -10.5,0 -12.5,4.5 -11,4.5\"/></g><path d=\"M40.0,76.0 L96.0,108.0 L130.0,90.0 L160.0,106.0 L176.0,98.0 L190.0,105.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\"/><circle cx=\"190.0\" cy=\"105.0\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"serif\" font-style=\"italic\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">plain: some point</text><circle cx=\"140.0\" cy=\"75.0\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"67\" font-size=\"8.5\" font-family=\"serif\" font-style=\"italic\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">Dykstra: true P</text><path d=\"M40.0,76.0 L140.0,75.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"9\" font-size=\"6.8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">y\u2096 = P\u2081(x\u2096 + p\u2096),  p\u2096\u208a\u2081 = x\u2096 + p\u2096 \u2212 y\u2096  (same for C\u2082 with q)</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "Plain alternating projection lands on some point of C\u2081 \u2229 C\u2082. Dykstra adds back the correction it removed last time, y\u2096 = P\u2081(x\u2096 + p\u2096) with p\u2096\u208a\u2081 = x\u2096 + p\u2096 \u2212 y\u2096 (and the same with q for C\u2082), and so reaches the true projection onto C\u2081 \u2229 C\u2082.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>The Challenge:</strong> Projecting onto the intersection $C_1 \\cap C_2$ when individual projections $\\Pi_{C_1}$ and $\\Pi_{C_2}$ are easy, but $\\Pi_{C_1 \\cap C_2}$ has no closed form.",
      "<strong>Von Neumann vs. Dykstra:</strong> Simple alternating projection $\\dots \\Pi_2(\\Pi_1(\\Pi_2(x))) \\dots$ finds AN arbitrary point in $C_1 \\cap C_2$, but NOT the closest projection $\\Pi_{C_1 \\cap C_2}(x)$!",
      "<strong>Dykstra's Innovation:</strong> Subtracts the outward normal correction vector before projecting at each step: $y_k = \\Pi_{C_1}(x_k + p_k)$, $p_{k+1} = x_k + p_k - y_k$, guaranteed to converge to the exact minimum Euclidean distance point."
    ],
    "math": "$$y_k=P_{C_1}(x_k+p_k),\\ p_{k+1}=x_k+p_k-y_k,\\quad x_{k+1}=P_{C_2}(y_k+q_k),\\ q_{k+1}=y_k+q_k-x_{k+1}$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>The airplane analogy.</strong> Two overlapping airspaces. Plain alternating ATC projections drift along the crease and end at some point in the overlap, not the nearest one. Dykstra's algorithm remembers the sideways correction from each bounce, so the plane lands on the closest point of the overlap.",
    "examTrap": "Exam questions love to ask: 'Does alternating projection find $\\Pi_{C_1 \\cap C_2}(x)$?' Answer: ONLY if $C_1, C_2$ are linear affine subspaces! For general convex sets, you MUST use Dykstra's normal correction.",
    "hint": "What does Dykstra's memory do that plain alternating projection does not?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$y_k=P_{C_1}(x_k+p_k)$:</strong> project onto the first airspace, but first add back the correction remembered from last time.",
      "<strong>$p_{k+1}=x_k+p_k-y_k$:</strong> remember what this projection moved you sideways by.",
      "<strong>Without these memory vectors you end at some point of the overlap;</strong> with them you end at the nearest point of the overlap."
    ],
    "stmtKind": "ALGORITHM",
    "statement": "<b>Dykstra's algorithm</b> (projection onto $C_1\\cap C_2$, both closed convex). Set $x_0=p$, $p_0=q_0=0$. Repeat: $y_k=P_{C_1}(x_k+p_k)$, $p_{k+1}=x_k+p_k-y_k$, $x_{k+1}=P_{C_2}(y_k+q_k)$, $q_{k+1}=y_k+q_k-x_{k+1}$. Then $x_k\\to P_{C_1\\cap C_2}(p)$.",
